## Migration: BackgroundProcessor → .NET 8

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while (_running)` + `Thread.Abort` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` |
| `GetNextItem()` blocking | Blocking call interruption | Async + token, or `WaitHandle` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Grace period | `Thread.Join(timeout)` |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — same logic that was in the
                // ThreadAbortException catch block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed; we simply continue to exit the loop
                // since the while condition will be false.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't exit in time. With cooperative cancellation
            // this means GetNextItem or ProcessItem isn't responding to
            // the token — log a warning. You cannot force-kill it.
            Log.Warning("Worker thread did not exit within timeout.");
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes to `GetNextItem`

The original could block indefinitely. You **must** make it cancellation-aware:

```csharp
// If it uses a BlockingCollection<T>:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}

// If it uses a WaitHandle/ManualResetEvent:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    int index = WaitHandle.WaitAny(
        new[] { _itemAvailable, token.WaitHandle });

    if (index == 1)
        throw new OperationCanceledException(token);

    return DequeueItem();
}
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing token into blocking calls** | If `GetNextItem()` ignores the token, `Cancel()` won't unblock it and `Stop()` will hang past the timeout. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated cancellations. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Implement `IDisposable`. |
| **Expecting immediate stop** | Cooperative cancellation only works at checkpoints. If `ProcessItem` is a long CPU-bound operation, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside it at regular intervals. |
| **Replacing `Thread.Abort` with `Thread.Interrupt`** | Also obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9). Use `CancellationToken` only. |

---

### If `ProcessItem` Is Long-Running

Add checkpoints inside it:

```csharp
private void ProcessItem(WorkItem item, CancellationToken token)
{
    foreach (var step in item.Steps)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        ExecuteStep(step);
    }
}
```

This ensures the thread responds to cancellation within one iteration rather than waiting for the entire item to finish.
